## The Science of Spa

Spa culture is often presented as a matter of tradition and feeling — and it is both of those things. But the mineral springs of Marienbad are also a subject of serious science. In this series, we explore the research behind the treatments: how CO₂ baths affect blood circulation, what happens to the body during a peat wrap, why drinking mineral water in prescribed doses can influence digestion and metabolism, and what modern balneology (the science of therapeutic bathing) has to say about age-old practices.

### Featured Topics

- [**How CO₂ Baths Work**](/en/magazine/co2-baths-science) — The physiology of carbon dioxide absorption through the skin, and why a simple bath can produce such profound effects on circulation and blood pressure.
- [**Drinking Cure Guide**](/en/magazine/drinking-cure-guide) — What makes each spring unique: a guide to the key minerals, their concentrations and their documented effects on the body.
- [**Musculoskeletal Treatment with Peat and Mineral Water**](/en/magazine/musculoskeletal-treatment-peat-mineral) — The humic acids, trace elements and thermal properties that make Bohemian peat a uniquely effective therapeutic material.
- [**Burnout Prevention at the Spa**](/en/magazine/burnout-prevention-spa) — Why the spa environment itself — the pace, the beauty, the ritual — contributes to healing in ways that science is only beginning to understand.

